export const html = "<h1 id=\"chapter-9--accounts-and-authentication\">Chapter 9 · Accounts and Authentication</h1>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How you get an account on Overhype.me, the order a new account goes\nthrough before it can act, what \"signed in\" means day to day, and what\nhappens when an account is <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#suspend\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">suspended</a> or\n<a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#account-deletion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">deleted</a>.</p>\n<p>Deep spec: <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/accounts-and-auth.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\"><code>accounts-and-auth.md</code></a>.\nSecurity posture (trust boundaries, session security): <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/security-model.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\"><code>security-model.md</code></a>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-does\">What it does</h2>\n<p>Overhype.me needs to know who you are any time an action is tied to a\nperson rather than just a page — submitting a fact, saving a meme, rating\nor commenting, and paying for a membership all require being signed in.\nNothing else does: browsing, personalizing what you see, and sharing a\nmeme all work without an account.</p>\n<p>Since launch Phase 5, an account is a <strong>verified, consenting, age-known\nidentity that can leave cleanly</strong>: it has confirmed its email, told us its\ndate of birth and pronouns, recorded its marketing choice, and can be\ndeleted — by its owner or by an admin — without leaving a mess behind.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works</h2>\n<h3 id=\"signing-in\">Signing in</h3>\n<p>You can create an account with Google, with Apple, or with an email and\npassword — all three end up in the same place: a signed-in session that\nlooks identical to the rest of the product no matter which door you came\nthrough. There's no \"sign in via a link we email you\" option. The emails\nthat touch sign-in are a one-time verification message, sent after you\nregister with email and password or change your email address, and a reset\nlink if you've forgotten your password. Neither is a standing way to log\nin.</p>\n<p>Google and Apple tell us whether they have confirmed your email. If they\nhave, your account counts as verified. If they haven't, and the email\nalready belongs to an account here, the sign-in is refused and you're\npointed at password sign-in or a password reset instead. Matching on an\nunconfirmed email would hand that account to whoever controls the\nunconfirmed address.</p>\n<p>If you signed up with Google or Apple and later want to also be able to log\nin with a password, you can set one from your account settings without being\nasked to prove you know a password you never had — or follow a reset link,\nwhich works for an account that has no password yet.</p>\n<h3 id=\"verify-then-onboard-then-act\">Verify, then onboard, then act</h3>\n<p>Every new account goes through the same order, whichever way it signed up,\nand the server enforces it on every write. The browser only steers you to\nthe right screen.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#email-verification\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">Verify your email</a>.</strong> Until\nyou do, you can sign in and look around, but nothing you try to do —\nsubmit, comment, rate, upload — goes through. (A Google or Apple sign-up\nwhose provider confirmed the email skips this step.)</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#onboarding\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">Onboard</a>.</strong> One screen for every\nsign-in method asks for your date of birth, confirms your pronouns and\nname, and records your marketing choice. The box is pre-ticked for\nvisitors in the US and unticked everywhere else. It also asks you to\npass one Cloudflare Turnstile challenge (proving you're a person). If\nyou leave half-way, your answers are kept for next time. <strong>Under 13 is\nrefused</strong>: nothing is stored and nothing is destroyed. Accounts that\nexisted before Phase 5 are asked once too.</li>\n<li><strong>Act.</strong> From then on the account can do everything its membership\nallows. Adults (18 and over, worked out from the date of birth each time\nit is needed, never stored as a flag) are also offered a profile photo,\nand only adults can upload photos at all.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There's no separate \"guest\" or \"anonymous\" tier of account — every account\nyou can sign in as is a full account, not a lighter stand-in. (Admin-created\naccounts are a separate case — an <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#admin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">admin</a> can\ncreate an account that starts genuinely\n<a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#unregistered\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">unregistered</a> rather than at the\nnormal default, which is a membership state, not something onboarding\ncontrols.)</p>\n<p>A sign-up that never finishes onboarding is cleaned up: after a month or\nso it is scheduled for deletion and its owner is emailed, with the usual\ncooling-off to keep it (below). That never happens to an account an\nadmin made, or to one that has already kept itself once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"resetting-or-changing-your-password\">Resetting or changing your password</h3>\n<p>Forgetting your password works the way you'd expect: request a reset, get an\nemail, follow the link, set a new password. Overhype.me always shows the\nsame confirmation after you request a reset, whether or not that email\nactually belongs to an account — so a reset request can't be used to check\nwho has an account here. A reset link works once: if two tabs use it at the\nsame moment, one changes the password and the other is told the link was\nused.</p>\n<p><strong>Every password change signs your other sessions out.</strong> A reset signs out\nevery device; setting or changing your password from your account settings\nsigns out every device but the one you're using. A password change is often a\nresponse to an account being compromised somewhere, so whoever else is\nsigned in as you shouldn't stay signed in.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-an-account-is-suspended\">When an account is suspended</h3>\n<p>An admin can <strong>suspend</strong> an account, giving a reason only admins ever see.\nSuspension signs the account out everywhere and stops it signing back in by\nany door — password, Google, Apple, or a reset link. A device that was\nsigned in can still look around, but everything it tries to do is refused\nand it's shown one screen: <em>\"This account is suspended.\"</em> It never says\nwhy.</p>\n<p>Suspension blocks the <strong>person</strong>, not the catalogue: the account's facts,\nmemes and comments stay live, and its membership billing is left alone. An\nadmin can lift it at any time, and the account carries on as before; a\nreset link requested before the suspension still works afterwards, if it\nhasn't expired. An admin\ncan't suspend their own account, or the last administrator who can reach\nthe console.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-an-account-is-deleted\">When an account is deleted</h3>\n<p><strong>You can delete your own account</strong> from your profile (\"Delete my\naccount…\"). Because it should be hard to do by accident, it asks for your\npassword first — or, for an account that only signs in with Google or\nApple, a sign-in made just now. Then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>You're signed out everywhere at once, and your memes, photos and\ngenerated images are hidden at once</strong> — though someone who already loaded\none may still see it for a short while. Your facts and comments stay\nreadable meanwhile (what happens to them at the end is below). Cancelling\nany paid subscription starts at the same moment, and if Stripe doesn't\nconfirm it, it is retried until it does. You're emailed.</li>\n<li><strong>You have a cooling-off period to change your mind</strong> (its length is in\nthe <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/accounts-and-auth.md#account-deletion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">spec</a>). Signing back in takes you to\none screen with a <strong>\"Keep my account\"</strong> button; pressing it brings your\naccount and content back. Anything you deleted by hand before then stays\ndeleted.</li>\n<li><strong>When the cooling-off ends, the account is deleted.</strong> Your photos, memes\nand generated images are removed from Overhype.me (kept for a short while\nin a backup, as every deleted photo is). Your account goes too. What you gave\nthe community stays, without your name: <strong>facts persist forever</strong>,\ncredited to a <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#former-member\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">former member</a>. So do\nyour comments, your submissions waiting for review, the links you added,\nand your votes and hearts in the totals.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>An admin can <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#remove\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">remove</a> an account at\nonce</strong>, from the user editor. That is the abuse case, so it goes further: the\naccount's comments, submissions waiting for review, links, votes and hearts\nare deleted, and the totals they fed are recounted. Facts still persist,\ncredited to a former member. If the member had already asked to delete their\nown account, the admin's removal takes it over.</p>\n<p>Admins see every deletion in progress on one panel, with when it will\nhappen and anything that went wrong. \"Purge now\" brings a member's own\ndeletion forward, keeping what it keeps; on a deletion that failed\npart-way, it is also the retry. Admins can also keep an account on its\nowner's behalf, and the owner is emailed. A billing dispute pauses the\ndeletion until it's settled. Anything tied to a legal or safety report\nkeeps its record — the report row and its evidence survive the deletion —\nthough not who it was tied to.</p>\n<h3 id=\"deleting-things-from-my-content\">Deleting things from My content</h3>\n<p>Separately from deleting your whole account: anything you delete from <strong>My\ncontent</strong> — an upload, a generated image, a meme — moves to <strong>Recently\ndeleted</strong> for a month or so and comes back with one click. After that it is\nremoved. While it waits there, only you can see it on the site, though\nsomeone who already loaded it may still see their copy for a short while.</p>\n<h3 id=\"staying-signed-in\">Staying signed in</h3>\n<p>Once you're signed in, Overhype.me keeps you that way across visits without\nasking you to log in again every time — there's no separate \"remember me\"\nchoice to make, that's just how a normal session behaves. You can be signed\nin on more than one device at once — a phone and a laptop, say — and signing\nin on a new one doesn't sign the others out. Some events sign out every\ndevice at once: a password change (all but the device making it, when you\nchange it yourself), a suspension, and a request to delete the account.</p>\n<h3 id=\"who-counts-as-what\">Who counts as what</h3>\n<p>Overhype.me distinguishes a handful of standing <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#role\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">roles</a> — signed-out visitor,\n<a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#unregistered\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">Unregistered</a> (an admin-created starting state, not the normal default),\n<a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#registered\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">registered</a> user, <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#legendary\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">Legendary</a> member, admin — and which one applies to you is\nalways figured out fresh from your actual account state, never something\nstored on your session that could go stale or be tampered with. The same is\ntrue of whether you're verified, onboarded, suspended or leaving. For the\nstandard admin gate the product relies on almost everywhere, an admin who\ntemporarily views the site \"<a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#view-as-regular-user\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">as a regular user</a>\" (to check what a member\nactually sees) never actually loses their admin permissions on the backend\nwhile doing it — that toggle only changes what the interface shows them.\nA handful of narrower, more mechanical endpoints (scheduled-job triggers\nand similar) check the toggle directly rather than going through that\nstandard gate, so those specifically would treat a viewing-as-user admin\nas a non-admin for as long as the toggle stays on — a known inconsistency\nin a small corner, not how authorization works generally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-it-works-this-way\">Why it works this way</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One order, enforced in one place.</strong> Verify, then onboard, then act is\nchecked by a single gate on every write, with one table saying what each\nkind of not-yet-ready account may still do. A rule spread across every\nscreen would have a screen that forgot it.</li>\n<li><strong>Suspension and deletion are different things.</strong> Suspension stops a\nperson and is undone by an admin; deletion is a person leaving, on a clock\nthey control. They used to share one \"inactive\" switch, which meant the\nproduct couldn't tell a banned account from a departing one. That switch\nis retired (the admin \"deactivate\" and \"reinstate\" actions went with it),\nand each state has its own record.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaving cleanly respects the community and the person.</strong> What someone\ngave others — a fact, a comment, a vote — stays when they leave, without\ntheir name, as on Reddit and Stack Overflow. Their own things — photos,\nmemes, generated images — go. An admin removing an abusive account is the\nexception, so it takes the contributions too (David, 2026-11-02).</li>\n<li><strong>A deletion is hard to do by accident and easy to undo for a while.</strong>\nAsking for the password (or a fresh sign-in) stops a borrowed device or a\nslip of the finger; the cooling-off stops a moment of anger.</li>\n<li><strong>Signing out every device is reserved for real signals.</strong> A password\nchange, a suspension and a deletion request each mean whoever else is\nsigned in as you shouldn't be. Ordinary actions affect only the session\nthey happen on.</li>\n<li><strong>Roles and account states are computed, never trusted from the\nsession.</strong> Storing \"this session belongs to an admin\" or \"this account is\nallowed to act\" on the session would let a stale or tampered session keep\na permission after it was taken away.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"boundaries--known-limitations\">Boundaries &#x26; known limitations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No Terms acknowledgement yet.</strong> Onboarding records marketing consent\nonly; Phase 14 adds the Terms and Privacy pages and asks every account\nonce (David overrode the recommendation to add minimal text now).</li>\n<li><strong>Adult content isn't on.</strong> The data model can mark facts, memes and\nimages as adult, and record an adult verification, but nothing uses\neither yet. The old opt-in for NSFW images is withdrawn, so a photo the\nclassifier flags is refused for everyone.</li>\n<li><strong>A member can't yet delete one of their own comments.</strong> That comes in\nPhase 9, before launch.</li>\n<li><strong>A suspended member who had asked to delete their account can't keep\nit</strong>, since keeping is an action a suspended account can't take.</li>\n<li><strong>A change that lands in the very instant a suspension does</strong> — a reset's\nnew password, a verification — can still be stored. It leaves no session\nand no new capability, so it ends where a moment-earlier action would\nhave.</li>\n<li><strong>Concurrent sign-ins are unlimited.</strong> Overhype.me doesn't cap how many\ndevices or sessions one account can be signed into at once, and doesn't\nshow you a list of them to review or revoke individually.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"going-deeper\">Going deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Spec: <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/accounts-and-auth.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\"><code>accounts-and-auth.md</code></a> — the\nexact routes, the account states and the gate, session mechanics, token\nhandling, suspension, and the deletion engine's stages.</li>\n<li>Security posture: <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/security-model.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\"><code>security-model.md</code></a> —\ntrust boundaries, session security properties, rate limits.</li>\n<li>Related: <a href=\"/admin/help/10-payments-and-membership\" data-help-internal=\"/admin/help/10-payments-and-membership\"><code>10-payments-and-membership.md</code></a> (how\na role and <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/glossary.md#membership-tier\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\">membership tier</a> translate into what you can do and pay for).</li>\n<li>Rationale: the auth and account entries in <a href=\"https://github.com/TheAnswerManIsHere/Overhypeme/blob/main/docs/ai-context/decisions.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external=\"true\"><code>decisions.md</code></a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Next:</strong> chapter 10 — <a href=\"/admin/help/10-payments-and-membership\" data-help-internal=\"/admin/help/10-payments-and-membership\"><code>10-payments-and-membership.md</code></a>,\nfree vs. Legendary, plan shapes, and what a membership unlocks.</p>\n<p><em>Verified against <code>54f5b2f</code> (2026-11-02) · rewritten for launch Phase 5 (#795).</em></p>";
